✅ Reusable template (covers 80% problems)
🧠 4–5 sliding window patterns (THIS is the real intuition)
🎯 How to identify which pattern to use in interview
🚀 1. UNIVERSAL SLIDING WINDOW TEMPLATE
🔹 A. Fixed Window (like your problem)

Use when:

window size is fixed (like permutation / anagram)

int[] need = new int[26];
int[] window = new int[26];

// fill need
for (char c : s1.toCharArray()) {
    need[c - 'a']++;
}

// initial window
for (int i = 0; i < k; i++) {
    window[s2.charAt(i) - 'a']++;
}

// check initial match
int matches = 0;
for (int i = 0; i < 26; i++) {
    if (need[i] == window[i]) matches++;
}

int left = 0;

for (int right = k; right < s2.length(); right++) {

    if (matches == 26) return true;

    // ➕ add
    int r = s2.charAt(right) - 'a';
    window[r]++;
    if (window[r] == need[r]) matches++;
    else if (window[r] == need[r] + 1) matches--;

    // ➖ remove
    int l = s2.charAt(left) - 'a';
    window[l]--;
    if (window[l] == need[l]) matches++;
    else if (window[l] == need[l] - 1) matches--;

    left++;
}

return matches == 26;
🔹 B. Variable Window (most common)

Use when:

“longest/shortest substring” with condition

int left = 0;

for (int right = 0; right < n; right++) {

    // expand window
    add(s[right]);

    while (window invalid) {
        remove(s[left]);
        left++;
    }

    // update answer
    updateAns();
}
🧠 2. ONLY 5 TYPES OF SLIDING WINDOW (MASTER THIS)

This is GOLD. Everything fits into these.

🔥 TYPE 1: Fixed Size Window

👉 size = k

Examples:

Permutation in string
Max sum subarray of size k

Pattern:

expand → shrink immediately
🔥 TYPE 2: Longest Valid Substring

👉 maximize length

Examples:

Longest substring without repeating chars
At most K distinct chars

Pattern:

expand → shrink until valid → update max
🔥 TYPE 3: Shortest Valid Substring

👉 minimize length

Examples:

Minimum window substring

Pattern:

expand → once valid → shrink as much as possible
🔥 TYPE 4: Count Subarrays

👉 count how many valid windows

Examples:

Subarrays with sum ≤ k
Nice subarrays

Pattern:

count += (right - left + 1)
🔥 TYPE 5: Exact Match (Your Case)

👉 frequency match

Examples:

Anagrams
Permutation in string

Pattern:

frequency + matches tracking
🎯 3. HOW TO IDENTIFY IN INTERVIEW

Ask yourself:

❓ Q1: Is window size fixed?
YES → Type 1 or Type 5
❓ Q2: Is it longest/shortest?
Longest → Type 2
Shortest → Type 3
❓ Q3: Is it counting number of subarrays?
YES → Type 4
❓ Q4: Is it frequency/permutation?
YES → Type 5 (your current problem)
🧠 BUILD INTUITION (THIS IS KEY)

Instead of memorizing code, think:

🔥 Sliding Window = Controlled Expansion + Correction
RIGHT pointer → explore
LEFT pointer → fix mistakes
⚖️ Mental Model
Expand → “Let’s try including this”
Shrink → “Oops, invalid → fix it”
